      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I understand the reasoning behind this proposal, and I can see legitimate use cases for a Friends Only option. For example, someone might create an avatar for a small group of friends and want everyone to use the same upload without having to distribute Unity projects.\n\nHowever, I don't think avatar visibility should evolve into an increasingly granular permission system with Friends Only, Group Only, individual whitelists, multiple authorized groups, etc.\n\nPublic and Private already have a clear purpose. If an avatar is Public, you're choosing to make it available for other people to use. If you don't want other people using it, you keep it Private.\n\nFriends Only could be a reasonable convenience, but going further creates an entire access-control system. What happens when someone is unfriended while using the avatar? When they leave an authorized group? When a creator removes someone from a whitelist? Does the avatar disappear from favorites? Are they immediately switched out of it? What about multiple authorized groups? These aren't impossible problems, but they add complexity to solve increasingly niche cases.\n\nI also don't think selling avatars is a strong justification for this. VRChat already has an official Avatar Marketplace specifically designed to sell avatars and grant access to buyers. Using whitelists or groups as an unofficial entitlement system would partially duplicate something that already exists.\n\nAnd I think there's an important distinction between sharing and indexing. If the real problem is that creators are fine with people using their Public avatars but don't want third-party databases indexing them, then indexing is the issue that should be addressed. Redefining avatar visibility is a much broader solution.\n\nI wouldn't necessarily oppose a simple Friends Only option, but I think that's where the line should be. Public should mean publicly usable, Private should mean uploader-only, and the Marketplace should handle commercial distribution. Turning avatar visibility into a full permission hierarchy feels unnecessary for the actual problems being discussed.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
